---
// The single most important accuracy point on the site: three different "certificates".
---
<section class="container-x py-14">
  <p class="eyebrow">Plain-English guide</p>
  <h2 class="h2 mt-2">Three things people call “ISO certification”</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-3">
    <div class="card">
      <p class="font-semibold">1. Your organisation’s certificate</p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">Issued by an independent, accredited <strong>certification body</strong> after it audits your management system. We help you get ready — we don’t issue it, and nobody can honestly guarantee the result.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <p class="font-semibold">2. A person’s training certificate</p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">Earned by an individual who completes a course such as Internal Auditor or Lead Auditor. Where a course carries a specific accreditation or exam, the course details state it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <p class="font-semibold">3. Laboratory accreditation</p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">Testing and calibration labs are <strong>accredited</strong> to ISO/IEC 17025 by a national accreditation body — EIAC in the UAE, SAAC in Saudi Arabia — rather than certified.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
